1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,500
We accept God as the efficient cause (nimitta karana).

2
00:00:04,500 --> 00:00:09,500
But we do not accept Him as the material cause (upadana karana),

3
00:00:09,500 --> 00:00:13,500
nor do we accept the theory of transformation (parinamavada).

4
00:00:13,500 --> 00:00:16,500
The reason for this is quite clear.

5
00:00:16,500 --> 00:00:21,500
In the case of divine forms, for example, there is the Matsya (fish) incarnation.

6
00:00:21,500 --> 00:00:25,500
You gave the analogy of metal.

7
00:00:25,500 --> 00:00:30,500
You mentioned that the same metal becomes an idol, and that both are indeed metal.

8
00:00:30,500 --> 00:00:35,500
This metal analogy fits well with the divine forms of God,

9
00:00:35,500 --> 00:00:41,500
but how do you apply this to Prakriti (insentient nature) and to God?

10
00:00:41,500 --> 00:00:46,500
To be more precise, in the incarnations of Rama and Krishna,

11
00:00:46,500 --> 00:00:49,500
Krishna Himself appeared as Rama.

12
00:00:49,500 --> 00:00:54,500
One can say that He transformed (parinamisida) into Rama.

13
00:00:54,500 --> 00:00:59,500
In the metal analogy, the same metal again becomes something else;

14
00:00:59,500 --> 00:01:03,500
we can say it underwent transformation.

15
00:01:03,500 --> 00:01:07,500
Therefore, there is non-difference (abheda) between Krishna and Rama.

16
00:01:07,500 --> 00:01:13,500
The same Supreme Soul has ten incarnations, thousands of forms, and infinite manifestations.

17
00:01:13,500 --> 00:01:17,500
It is in this manner that we say He pervades the entire universe.

18
00:01:17,500 --> 00:01:21,500
I believe you agree with this as well.

19
00:01:21,500 --> 00:01:25,500
Furthermore, when it comes to the topic of creation,

20
00:01:25,500 --> 00:01:29,500
it involves both the sentient (chetana) and the insentient (jada).

21
00:01:29,500 --> 00:01:35,500
A major question arises there: how can God transform into both sentient and insentient entities?

22
00:01:35,500 --> 00:01:41,500
Moreover, the analogy of the spider and the web that you provided is found in the Upanishads.

23
00:01:41,500 --> 00:01:45,500
However, the spider is different from the web.

24
00:01:45,500 --> 00:01:51,500
The universe, which consists of both the sentient and the insentient, resides within God's belly itself.

25
00:01:51,500 --> 00:01:56,500
It resides within His belly, yet it is distinct from God.

26
00:01:56,500 --> 00:02:02,500
But 'parinama' (transformation) implies change in the sense of transformation.

27
00:02:02,500 --> 00:02:06,500
That would mean asserting non-difference, wouldn't it?

28
00:02:06,500 --> 00:02:10,500
I feel it is like asserting non-difference.

29
00:02:10,500 --> 00:02:15,500
Because, in a transformation (parinama), the metal is not different from the idol; both are one and the same.

30
00:02:15,500 --> 00:02:19,500
For instance, the copper metal is not different from the copper idol.

31
00:02:19,500 --> 00:02:23,500
That very metal transformed to become the idol.

32
00:02:23,500 --> 00:02:27,500
We can apply this meaning to the forms of God.

33
00:02:27,500 --> 00:02:31,500
In a sense, we can say this to some extent.

34
00:02:31,500 --> 00:02:36,500
Of course, no analogy can perfectly depict or prove God.

35
00:02:36,500 --> 00:02:42,500
Nevertheless, for our limited intellect to comprehend God, we somehow explain it in this manner.

36
00:02:42,500 --> 00:02:46,500
Furthermore, a spider has limbs, a form, and so forth.

37
00:02:46,500 --> 00:02:51,500
But the spider's web is not alive; the two are different—one is insentient (jada),

38
00:02:51,500 --> 00:02:54,500
and the spider is sentient (chetana).

39
00:02:54,500 --> 00:02:58,500
Therefore, the two are distinct and different from each other.

40
00:02:58,500 --> 00:03:04,500
If we say God transformed, according to us, it means saying God transformed into the universe

41
00:03:04,500 --> 00:03:09,500
is like saying Matsya became Kurma, and Kurma became Narasimha.

42
00:03:09,500 --> 00:03:15,500
We can say this regarding the forms of God, but how can we say Narayana Himself became the universe?

43
00:03:15,500 --> 00:03:19,500
How can we speak of transformation in that manner?

44
00:03:19,500 --> 00:03:25,500
How can we say Narayana Himself transformed into a universe of both insentient and sentient entities?

45
00:03:25,500 --> 00:03:31,500
If we say that even once, it means asserting a transformation (parinama) between Narayana and this universe,

46
00:03:31,500 --> 00:03:36,500
which is composed of both the insentient and the living (sentient).

47
00:03:36,500 --> 00:03:41,500
That would be like saying the universe is Narayana's body.

48
00:03:41,500 --> 00:03:46,500
And if we say it is Narayana's body, doesn't it amount to asserting non-difference (abheda)?

49
00:03:46,500 --> 00:03:52,500
If we are to assert non-difference, we should assert it between Matsya and Kurma,

50
00:03:52,500 --> 00:03:56,500
or between Matsya and Narasimha.

51
00:03:56,500 --> 00:04:02,500
We say that Matsya Himself became Narasimha, or Narasimha became Vamana, or Vamana became Parashurama—

52
00:04:02,500 --> 00:04:07,500
applying this to the ten incarnations, or the thousands of forms, and so on.

53
00:04:07,500 --> 00:04:13,500
But how can we apply this theory of transformation (parinamavada) to this distinct universe

54
00:04:13,500 --> 00:04:17,500
made of living and non-living entities?

55
00:04:17,500 --> 00:04:22,500
It does not fit; we feel that such an interpretation is incorrect.

56
00:04:22,500 --> 00:04:28,500
What then do we understand from the Shruti statement that declares God to be the material cause (upadana karana)?

57
00:04:28,500 --> 00:04:33,500
What does it mean to say God became the universe?

58
00:04:33,500 --> 00:04:37,500
We interpret it simply to mean that He pervaded the entire universe.

59
00:04:37,500 --> 00:04:42,500
We cannot interpret it in a way that suggests identity (aikya) with this insentient world, can we?

60
00:04:42,500 --> 00:04:46,500
Another famous analogy is 'Navanari Kunjara, Panchanari Turaga'—

61
00:04:46,500 --> 00:04:52,500
meaning if nine women pose in a certain posture, they appear like an elephant.

62
00:04:52,500 --> 00:04:57,500
If five women pose in a certain posture, they appear like a horse.

63
00:04:57,500 --> 00:05:03,500
In the same way, God simply resides in a small child, assuming the child's form.

64
00:05:03,500 --> 00:05:07,500
But there is no intrinsic relation between the child and God.

65
00:05:07,500 --> 00:05:11,500
God's body is entirely different.

66
00:05:11,500 --> 00:05:16,500
The child's body is composed of blood and flesh, whereas God's body is non-material (aprakruta).

67
00:05:16,500 --> 00:05:21,500
Thus, we can only say He speaks like a child in that sense;

68
00:05:21,500 --> 00:05:25,500
otherwise, it would attribute defects to God.

69
00:05:25,500 --> 00:05:30,500
If God were to transform like this insentient and sentient world,

70
00:05:30,500 --> 00:05:34,500
it would attribute defects to Him.

71
00:05:34,500 --> 00:05:39,500
For the faultless Lord, the Shruti statements proclaiming His faultlessness would contradict this.

72
00:05:39,500 --> 00:05:43,500
How do you interpret this?

73
00:05:43,500 --> 00:05:47,500
[Second Speaker]
I have no disagreement with anything you have said.

74
00:05:47,500 --> 00:05:52,500
You said earlier that such defects would arise if we say Brahman undergoes transformation (parinama).

75
00:05:52,500 --> 00:05:57,500
I still maintain that the difference lies only in the mode of expression, not in the meaning.

76
00:05:57,500 --> 00:06:02,500
Because we fully accept whatever meaning you have articulated.

77
00:06:02,500 --> 00:06:06,500
Your name is Ashish, right? Okay, let's take 'Ramayya' as an example.

78
00:06:06,500 --> 00:06:10,500
Suppose there is a young man named Ramayya.

79
00:06:10,500 --> 00:06:14,500
If we say 'Ramayya became old,' does it not mean that Ramayya's body attained old age?

80
00:06:14,500 --> 00:06:18,500
Does it mean that Ramayya's soul changed or aged? No, right?

81
00:06:18,500 --> 00:06:22,500
That is exactly what we say.

82
00:06:22,500 --> 00:06:27,500
When we say Brahman became the universe or became the living beings,

83
00:06:27,500 --> 00:06:32,500
it is only His body—His attribute (visheshana)—that undergoes transformation, not His soul (atman).

84
00:06:32,500 --> 00:06:37,500
You are saying the same thing using different words when you say everything is inside the Lord's belly,

85
00:06:37,500 --> 00:06:41,500
and the source of the entire universe is there. We say the same.

86
00:06:41,500 --> 00:06:46,500
However, as the Shruti says, His body undergoes transformation in that very manner.

87
00:06:46,500 --> 00:06:51,500
Just as the Shruti has no hesitation in describing the body-soul relationship (shareera-shareeree bhava)

88
00:06:51,500 --> 00:06:55,500
between the universe and the Supreme Soul, neither do we.

89
00:06:55,500 --> 00:06:59,500
You are the one who hesitates; the Madhvas hesitate.

90
00:06:59,500 --> 00:07:03,500
We only say what the Shruti says.

91
00:07:03,500 --> 00:07:07,500
If you say we are wrong, it means the Shruti is wrong.

92
00:07:07,500 --> 00:07:11,500
If you agree that the Shruti is correct, then we are merely repeating its words.

93
00:07:11,500 --> 00:07:16,500
My point is that the difference is only in the expression; there is no difference in the fundamental reality.

94
00:07:16,500 --> 00:07:20,500
[First Speaker]
The body-soul relationship (shareera-shareeree bhava) that you speak of

95
00:07:20,500 --> 00:07:25,500
and the inner controller theory (antaryami vada) that we speak of,

96
00:07:25,500 --> 00:07:30,500
though different in words, carry the same import.

97
00:07:30,500 --> 00:07:34,500
Okay, that is correct. Let us assume it is correct in a sense.

98
00:07:34,500 --> 00:07:39,500
However, when you assert the theory of transformation (parinamavada),

99
00:07:39,500 --> 00:07:43,500
you must speak of the creation of the universe.

100
00:07:43,500 --> 00:07:48,500
That is, you have to explain how this universe of insentient and sentient entities was formed,

101
00:07:48,500 --> 00:07:51,500
how this world arose.

102
00:07:51,500 --> 00:07:55,500
This world is insentient.

103
00:07:55,500 --> 00:08:00,500
And we both share the same view that there are many living beings in this universe;

104
00:08:00,500 --> 00:08:03,500
there are infinite living beings.

105
00:08:03,500 --> 00:08:08,500
Did God Himself become the life of these infinite living beings?

106
00:08:08,500 --> 00:08:13,500
On one hand, you propound the theory of Him being the inner controller (antaryami).

107
00:08:13,500 --> 00:08:18,500
On the other hand, by asserting the theory of transformation (parinamavada),

108
00:08:18,500 --> 00:08:23,500
you imply that God became a chair, or God became a living being, or God became a table—

109
00:08:23,500 --> 00:08:27,500
as transformation (parinama) means change.

110
00:08:27,500 --> 00:08:32,500
Since you gave the analogy of metal becoming an idol, this analogy does not fit the universe.

111
00:08:32,500 --> 00:08:37,500
The reason is that the universe is insentient, while God is supremely sentient.

112
00:08:37,500 --> 00:08:42,500
Since there is a distinction between the supremely sentient and the insentient,

113
00:08:42,500 --> 00:08:46,500
how can God transform into the insentient?

114
00:08:46,500 --> 00:08:50,500
Or how can God become an ordinary living being? That is my question.

115
00:08:50,500 --> 00:08:55,500
In a way, as I said, there is no disagreement between you and us; it is only a difference in words.

116
00:08:55,500 --> 00:09:00,500
But in this theory of transformation (parinamavada), doesn't it end up asserting non-difference (abheda)?

117
00:09:00,500 --> 00:09:05,500
That is, we say God is only the efficient cause (nimitta karana).

118
00:09:05,500 --> 00:09:10,500
What this means is that God is only the efficient cause, like a potter.

119
00:09:10,500 --> 00:09:15,500
Being the inner controller of all, He remains solely the efficient cause;

120
00:09:15,500 --> 00:09:19,500
He is not the material cause (upadana karana) as the inner controller of all.

121
00:09:19,500 --> 00:09:24,500
If you say He is both the inner controller of all and also the material cause,

122
00:09:24,500 --> 00:09:28,500
how is that possible? They contradict each other.

123
00:09:28,500 --> 00:09:33,500
The theory of transformation (parinamavada) and the theory of the inner controller (antaryami vada) would collide.

124
00:09:33,500 --> 00:09:37,500
I say it once again: we accept Him only as the efficient cause.

125
00:09:37,500 --> 00:09:41,500
He is the inner controller in the universe,

126
00:09:41,500 --> 00:09:46,500
but He is the inner controller solely as the efficient cause.

127
00:09:46,500 --> 00:09:51,500
God is absolutely not the material cause of this universe.

128
00:09:51,500 --> 00:09:56,500
Why? Because to be the material cause, He must undergo transformation (parinama).

129
00:10:01,500 --> 00:10:06,500
But God never transforms into the universe.

130
00:10:06,500 --> 00:10:11,500
You have already accepted that God is the efficient cause; there is no dispute about that.

131
00:10:11,500 --> 00:10:16,500
In that regard, as you said, both views align;

132
00:10:16,500 --> 00:10:21,500
the inner controller theory and the body-soul relationship carry the same import by accepting Him as the efficient cause.

133
00:10:21,500 --> 00:10:26,500
But if you say God is also the material cause, meaning He undergoes transformation,

134
00:10:26,500 --> 00:10:31,500
and if He indeed transforms, then the question arises: how should He transform?

135
00:10:31,500 --> 00:10:36,500
Matsya should transform into Kurma, Kurma into Varaha, Varaha into Narasimha.

136
00:10:36,500 --> 00:10:41,500
Why should Varaha become a living being of this universe, an ordinary living being?

137
00:10:41,500 --> 00:10:46,500
Or why should the Supreme Lord in the form of Varaha become this ordinary chair?

138
00:10:46,500 --> 00:10:51,500
Or why should He become an iron pillar?

139
00:10:51,500 --> 00:10:55,500
Or why should He become other objects? That is our only point of contention.

140
00:16:17,000 --> 00:16:22,000
[Second Speaker]
As you say, God's soul (essential nature) never becomes insentient.

141
00:16:22,000 --> 00:16:27,000
The Supreme Lord Himself—His essential self—can never become insentient iron or soil.

142
00:16:27,000 --> 00:16:31,000
That comes from Prakriti (nature), does it not?

143
00:16:31,000 --> 00:16:36,000
We are not proponents of essential transformation (swaroopa-parinamavada);

144
00:16:36,000 --> 00:16:40,000
we are proponents of attribute transformation (visheshana-parinamavada).

145
00:16:40,000 --> 00:16:45,000
By 'attribute' (visheshana), we clearly mean proponents of bodily transformation (shareera-parinamavada).

146
00:16:45,000 --> 00:16:50,000
For example, if we say 'Ramayya became lame,' we do not mean that Ramayya's soul became lame.

147
00:16:50,000 --> 00:16:55,000
Ramayya's body, which is distinct from Ramayya's soul, underwent that transformation.

148
00:16:55,000 --> 00:17:00,000
Similarly, when it is said 'Brahman became this clay,' we do not mean the Supreme Lord Himself became clay,

149
00:17:00,000 --> 00:17:05,000
but rather that Prakriti, which is the body of the Supreme Lord, became clay.

150
00:17:05,000 --> 00:17:10,000
Thus, the difference is only in the words, not in the meaning, because you also say the same.

151
00:17:10,000 --> 00:17:15,000
The transformations of Prakriti are the other insentient objects,

152
00:17:15,000 --> 00:17:19,000
and the transformations of the pre-existing souls are the current living beings.

153
00:17:19,000 --> 00:17:22,000
That is all; there is nothing more.

154
00:17:22,000 --> 00:17:27,000
In this way, the Supreme Lord Himself neither becomes this insentient Prakriti nor does He become a living being.

155
00:17:27,000 --> 00:17:31,000
Is that clear?

156
00:17:31,000 --> 00:17:35,000
[First Speaker]
Even if you do not accept essential transformation, by asserting attribute transformation,

157
00:17:35,000 --> 00:17:39,000
since according to us there is non-difference (abheda) between quality (guna) and the qualified (guni)...

158
00:17:39,000 --> 00:17:43,000
according to your view, it implies that the quality undergoes modification (vikara).

159
00:17:43,000 --> 00:17:48,000
If so, if the qualities undergo modification, then since there is non-difference between God's qualities and God Himself,

160
00:17:48,000 --> 00:17:52,000
it amounts to attributing modification (vikara) to God.

161
00:17:52,000 --> 00:17:56,000
Therefore, if you assert the theory of transformation (parinamavada), all these issues will arise; this is my view.

162
00:17:56,000 --> 00:18:01,000
According to our school, the process is such that there is indeed non-difference (abheda) between quality (guna) and the qualified (guni).

163
00:18:01,000 --> 00:18:05,000
However, only in practical usage (vyavahara) is there a distinction.

164
00:18:05,000 --> 00:18:09,000
For example, take jaggery and its sweetness.

165
00:18:09,000 --> 00:18:13,000
The sweetness of jaggery cannot be separated from the jaggery.

166
00:18:13,000 --> 00:18:16,000
This is called 'savisesha-abheda' (non-difference with specific distinctions).

167
00:18:16,000 --> 00:18:20,000
Even though there is non-difference, there is a distinction in practical usage;

168
00:18:20,000 --> 00:18:24,000
in practice alone is there a distinction.

169
00:18:24,000 --> 00:18:28,000
According to the body-soul relationship (shareera-shareeree bhava) of your school,

170
00:18:28,000 --> 00:18:32,000
since there is non-difference between God's body and God Himself,

171
00:18:32,000 --> 00:18:37,000
it is impossible for God to have this universe of insentient and sentient entities as His body.

172
00:18:37,000 --> 00:18:42,000
Because that non-difference of quality and the qualified applies only to His divine auspicious form (divya-mangala-vigraha).

173
00:18:42,000 --> 00:18:47,000
Between God's divine auspicious form and God Himself, there is non-difference of quality and the qualified.

174
00:18:47,000 --> 00:18:51,000
You can apply that system there.

175
00:18:51,000 --> 00:18:55,000
But here, the universe is a completely different entity and is distinct;

176
00:18:55,000 --> 00:19:00,000
therefore, asserting attribute transformation (visheshana-parinamavada) is incorrect, in my opinion.

177
00:19:00,000 --> 00:19:04,000
[Second Speaker]
It appears there is a confusion in your mind

178
00:19:04,000 --> 00:19:08,000
between the qualifier-qualified relationship (visheshana-visheshya bhava)

179
00:19:08,000 --> 00:19:12,000
and the quality-qualified relationship (guna-guni bhava).

180
00:19:12,000 --> 00:19:16,000
There is no rule that the qualifier (visheshana) of a qualified entity (visheshya) must only be a quality (guna);

181
00:19:16,000 --> 00:19:19,000
it can also be another substance (dravya).

182
00:19:19,000 --> 00:19:23,000
The qualifier-qualified relationship pertains to verbal expression (shabdabhivyakti).

183
00:19:23,000 --> 00:19:27,000
For example, my son can be my attribute/qualifier,

184
00:19:27,000 --> 00:19:31,000
or your profession at some point in time can be your attribute.

185
00:19:31,000 --> 00:19:35,000
But a quality is not like that. In saying 'I am fair-complexioned,'

186
00:19:35,000 --> 00:19:39,000
there is a quality-qualified relationship, but in expression, there is also a qualifier-qualified relationship.

187
00:19:39,000 --> 00:19:43,000
Similarly, one can also assert a qualifier-qualified relationship between an individual (vyakti) and a genus/class (jati).

188
00:19:43,000 --> 00:19:47,000
When we say 'the cow individual' (go-vyaktih), that cowness (gotva) is the class,

189
00:19:47,000 --> 00:19:51,000
and it serves as a qualifier (visheshana) for the individual.

190
00:19:51,000 --> 00:19:55,000
Likewise, when I say 'the cow class' (go-jatih),

191
00:19:55,000 --> 00:19:59,000
the individual cow also arises in the mind as a qualifier.

192
00:19:59,000 --> 00:20:03,000
Therefore, the qualifier-qualified relationship is not the same as the quality-qualified relationship.

193
00:20:03,000 --> 00:20:07,000
The qualifier-qualified relationship pertains to verbal expression, not to the essential meaning.

194
00:20:07,000 --> 00:20:11,000
Regarding the meaning, other relationships like the quality-qualified relationship,

195
00:20:11,000 --> 00:20:15,000
the body-soul relationship, etc., come into play.

196
00:20:15,000 --> 00:20:19,000
[First Speaker]
According to our school, only God's qualities can be qualifiers (visheshana) for God.

197
00:20:19,000 --> 00:20:23,000
No entity distinct from God can ever be called a qualifier (visheshana) for God.

198
00:20:23,000 --> 00:20:27,000
[Second Speaker]
You may add such inapplicable rules to the qualifier-qualified relationship

199
00:20:27,000 --> 00:20:31,000
and carry out your arguments accordingly; I have no objection.

200
00:20:31,000 --> 00:20:35,000
I only said that the meaning is the same.

201
00:20:35,000 --> 00:20:39,000
There is no obligation for others to follow the same rule.

202
00:18:17,000 --> 00:18:22,000
[First Speaker]
From your perspective, how can you say there is no entity other than Brahman?

203
00:18:22,000 --> 00:18:27,000
You yourself said that there is insentient matter, nature (Prakriti), and the living soul (Jiva);

204
00:18:27,000 --> 00:18:32,000
there are many entities, aren't there? How then can you speak in that manner now?

205
00:18:32,000 --> 00:18:38,000
If you say there is no entity other than Brahman, does it not become just like what the Advaitins say?

206
00:18:38,000 --> 00:18:43,000
[Second Speaker]
The Advaitins say there is no entity other than Brahman, and we say it too.

207
00:18:43,000 --> 00:18:47,000
But despite the similarity in words, the meaning is different.

208
00:18:47,000 --> 00:18:52,000
In fact, you are saying the same thing we say, but using different words.

209
00:18:52,000 --> 00:18:58,000
The meaning is the same; only the words are different. This difference exists.